GENERAL GUIDELINES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All commands are Linux shell commands unless explicitly identified as DXSpider
commands.
This guide covers a native installation using the
sysop account and the mojo branch. Use sudo where indicated, or su
- to become root. If working as root, omit sudo from administrative
command blocks. Commands intended for sysop must be run as that
account. If a step fails, stop and investigate the cause before continuing.



PART I. NEW INSTALLATION
================================================================================


1. SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS AND PREPARATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1.1 NEW INSTALLATION

1.1.1 As a USER WITH SUDO PRIVILEGES (terminal Linux):

Default values used in this guide:

Linux user: sysop
Linux group: spider
HOME: /home/sysop
Repository: /home/sysop/spider
Operational link: /spider
Git branch: mojo
Service: dxspider.service

These are values proposed by this guide, not mandatory software
defaults. Minimum software requirements:
Perl >= 5.16.1
Mojolicious >= 7.26, Git
gzip, process utilities and dependencies from section 3
systemd if using the service described here.

Indicative minimum hardware:
at least 1 CPU
1 GiB RAM
20 GB disk

History, databases and traffic may require considerably more disk space and
memory.
On Debian/Ubuntu/Raspberry Pi OS, install the tools BEFORE cloning:

   sudo apt-get update
   sudo apt-get install -y git iproute2 perl gzip procps
   command -v git && command -v ss && command -v perl

The spider group must exist before enabling the service; section 9 checks and
creates it.

   sudo -v
   command -v git
   id sysop || sudo useradd -m -s /bin/sh sysop
   getent passwd sysop
   test -d /home/sysop
   test ! -e /home/sysop/spider
   test ! -L /home/sysop/spider

Install Git during system preparation; do not continue without verifying it.
Keeping
  Git up to date is recommended.
  If /home/sysop/spider already exists, DO NOT use this section.
  Verify that the sysop HOME directory is /home/sysop before continuing.



2. CLONING THE REPOSITORY AND CREATING THE /SPIDER LINK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2.1 As SYSOP (log in with sudo -iu sysop or su - sysop):

   sudo -iu sysop
   id -un
   cd /home/sysop
   git clone --branch mojo --single-branch \
     git://scm.dxcluster.org/spider spider
   cd /home/sysop/spider
   test "$(git branch --show-current)" = mojo
   git remote -v
   git log -1 --oneline

  The 'id -un' command must display sysop.
  If you logged in using 'su - sysop', run the same commands
  starting with 'id -un' (without 'sudo -iu sysop').

2.2 Exit the SYSOP session with 'exit'. As a USER WITH SUDO PRIVILEGES:

   exit
   sudo -v
   if [ -e /spider ] || [ -L /spider ]; then
     echo '/spider already exists: no changes made.'
   else
     sudo ln -s /home/sysop/spider /spider
   fi
   ls -ld /spider
   readlink -f /home/sysop/spider

  If /spider already existed, verify its target before
  using it. Never automatically delete or replace /spider.


3. PERL DEPENDENCIES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3.1 REQUIREMENTS AND DIFFERENCES

- Perl >= 5.16.1 and Mojolicious >= 7.26
- Mojo core: EV, Mojolicious, JSON, JSON::XS,
  Data::Structure::Util, Math::Round, Net::CIDR::Lite, DBI,
 DBD::SQLite (the correct CPAN module name is NOT DBD::SQLite3).
- Utilities: Date::Parse, HTML::Parser,
  IO::Socket::INET6, Archive::Zip, Curses, Mail::Internet,
  Digest::SHA1, LWP::UserAgent, LWP::Parallel::UserAgent, DB_File.
  IO::Pty (dxweb-admin),
 Authen::SASL and Net::SMTP::SSL (DXReg).
  Email::Sender
- Common core Perl modules such as JSON::PP, Digest::SHA,
 HTTP::Tiny, IO::Compress::Gzip, Time::HiRes, etc. are checked
 using Perl; some distributions package them separately.
- Gtk/Gtk2 and Win32::API are for graphical applications or Windows:
 do NOT install them on a headless Linux node.
- A non-SQLite SQL backend may require another DBD driver.

3.2 OPTION A - APT (DEBIAN/UBUNTU/RASPBERRY PI OS)

USER: sysop, with sudo enabled. Linux terminal. Do
not enter these commands in the DXSpider console.

3.2.1 System tools and build packages:

   sudo apt-get update
   sudo apt-get install -y perl git iproute2 gzip procps build-essential \
     cpanminus libssl-dev zlib1g-dev

3.2.2 Mojo modules and common utilities:

   sudo apt-get install -y \
     libev-perl libmojolicious-perl libjson-perl \
     libjson-xs-perl libdata-structure-util-perl \
     libmath-round-perl libnet-cidr-lite-perl \
     libdbi-perl libdbd-sqlite3-perl libdb-file-perl \
     libtimedate-perl libhtml-parser-perl \
     libio-socket-inet6-perl libarchive-zip-perl \
     libcurses-perl libmailtools-perl libdigest-sha1-perl \
     libwww-perl libio-pty-perl libauthen-sasl-perl \
     libnet-smtp-ssl-perl libemail-sender-perl


3.2.3 Some auxiliary packages may not exist in every distribution release.

  If apt fails, STOP: do not assume everything was installed.
  Check the available package name with:

   apt-cache policy PACKAGE_NAME

3.2.4 LWP::Parallel::UserAgent dependency

  LWP::Parallel::UserAgent is used by perl/callbot.pl. If it is missing,
  install the module using the CPAN alternative below.

3.3 OPTION B - CPANM (PERL MODULES)

  USER: sysop, with sudo enabled. Linux terminal.
  This option replaces section 3.2.2, NOT section 3.2.1.

3.3.1 Install Perl, cpanminus, a compiler and OS libraries first.

  On Debian/Ubuntu/Raspberry Pi OS, follow section 3.2.1. On
  other distributions, install the equivalent native packages.

3.3.2 Install the modules required by both code trees:

   sudo cpanm \
     EV Mojolicious JSON JSON::XS Data::Structure::Util \
     Math::Round Net::CIDR::Lite DBI DBD::SQLite \
     Date::Parse HTML::Parser IO::Socket::INET6 \
     Archive::Zip Curses Mail::Internet Digest::SHA1 \
     LWP::UserAgent LWP::Parallel::UserAgent DB_File \
     IO::Pty Authen::SASL Net::SMTP::SSL Email::Sender

  CPANM runs distribution tests before installing. If a build
  fails, stop and review OS dependencies. Do not combine
  sections 3.2.2 and 3.3.2 unnecessarily: mixing apt and system-wide
  CPAN packages can complicate later upgrades and troubleshooting.

3.4 REQUIRED VERIFICATION

  USER: sysop. Linux terminal. sudo is not required.

   perl -e 'die "Perl >= 5.16.1 required\n" if $] < 5.016001'
   perl -MMojolicious=7.26 -e 'print "Mojolicious OK\n"'
   perl -MEV -MJSON -MJSON::XS -MData::Structure::Util -e 1
   perl -MMath::Round -MNet::CIDR::Lite -MDBI -MDBD::SQLite -e 1
   perl -MDate::Parse -MHTML::Parser -MIO::Socket::INET6 -e 1
   perl -MArchive::Zip -MCurses -MMail::Internet -e 1
   perl -MDigest::SHA1 -MLWP::UserAgent -MDB_File -e 1
   perl -MIO::Pty -MAuthen::SASL -MNet::SMTP::SSL -e 1
   perl -MEmail::Sender -e 1
   perl -MLWP::Parallel::UserAgent -e 1

   command -v git
   command -v gzip

  If any command fails, stop and investigate the missing module. These
  checks verify that modules load, not that all features work.

3.5 NOTES

  - Package installation uses sudo; DXSpider runs as sysop.
  - No branch, directory or Git repository change is required.
  - APT packages have not been validated on every Linux release.
  - Graphical GTK modules and Windows dependencies are excluded.
  - No node code or configuration is changed.


4. PREPARING DIRECTORIES AND TEMPLATES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For a new installation, the local directory may NOT exist
after cloning Git. Do not assume it already exists.

As sysop, from /spider, create it before copying files:

   mkdir -p local local_cmd local_data
   test ! -e local/DXVars.pm && \
     cp perl/DXVars.pm.issue local/DXVars.pm
   test ! -e local/Listeners.pm && \
     cp perl/Listeners.pm local/Listeners.pm

If any of these files already exists, DO NOT overwrite it.

Check ownership:

   ls -ld /spider /spider/local /spider/local_data
   ls -l local/DXVars.pm local/Listeners.pm


5. INITIAL NODE CONFIGURATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

As sysop, edit the file copied in the previous step:

   nano /spider/local/DXVars.pm

Configure the variables shown in the template:
- Exact node callsign ($mycall), including SSID where applicable.
- SYSOP callsign ($myalias), distinct from the node callsign.
- Name, QTH, locator, email and other relevant information.

Save the file. Do not edit perl/DXVars.pm.issue: it is the template.
Do not copy callsigns, addresses or credentials from the examples.


6. TCP LISTENER CONFIGURATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

As sysop, edit the file copied in the previous step:

   nano /spider/local/Listeners.pm

Listener lines in the template may be commented out. Enable
the appropriate IPv4 or IPv6 line by removing its initial
#. Check the address and port in the file. Do
not enable both without checking for conflicts on the
system. Example port: 7300/TCP (use the port actually configured).

Check whether the port is already in use from a Linux terminal:

   ss -ltn

Do not expose the port to the Internet without reviewing firewall rules.


7. INITIAL SYSOP CREATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

WARNING: perl/create_sysop.pl initializes DXUser records but does NOT
create or modify local/DXVars.pm or local/Listeners.pm. Configure both
files before running it.

DANGER: this script may destroy an existing user database. This
guide is ONLY for a new installation. Do not
confirm data deletion if an unexpected prompt appears.

As sysop, from /spider:

   cd /spider
   test -f local/DXVars.pm || exit 1
   test -f local/Listeners.pm || exit 1
   test ! -e local_data/dxusers.db || {
     echo "Existing DXUser database: STOP"; exit 1;
   }
   perl perl/create_sysop.pl

Follow the script messages. If it fails, stop and investigate;
do not rerun it blindly. Checking dxusers.db is
an initial precaution, not an exhaustive check for previous databases.


8. FIRST START AND CHECKS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

As sysop, with the DXSpider service stopped and no other cluster.pl running:

   cd /spider
   perl perl/cluster.pl

Keep this terminal open and inspect startup. If an error occurs,
do NOT create a service that restarts it in a
loop. Review paths, dependencies, DXVars.pm and Listeners.pm.

In another Linux terminal, check the port actually configured:

   ss -ltnp

Connect to the listening port using an appropriate client.
For example, use a local telnet client (if installed):

   telnet 127.0.0.1 7300

Port 7300 is ONLY an example: use the configured port. Verify
that the node responds and allows authentication as the expected SYSOP. Do
not assume the C client in src/ is required for TCP:
in mojo, the Perl process itself can handle Telnet connections.

8.1 BASIC OPERATIONAL TESTS

From the DXSpider SYSOP console, not from Linux:

   sh/version
   sh/cluster
   sh/connect

Verify that your own callsign is displayed and that
startup is error-free. Peers will not appear as connected until
configured. Check that debug output is being created under
/spider/local_data/debug.

To stop the interactive instance, enter this in the SYSOP console:

   shutdown

Verify that the process has stopped before enabling systemd.
In a Linux terminal, check that no cluster.pl process remains:

   pgrep -af '[p]erl.*cluster[.]pl' || true

If a process is listed, identify how it was started and stop it using the
corresponding procedure; do not enable systemd while it is
still running. Also check that the configured port is no longer listening:

   ss -ltnp

The port check is conclusive only if it uses the actual port configured in
Listeners.pm.


9. SYSTEMD SERVICE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

On a Linux system with systemd, use a service that runs
DXSpider as sysop. First verify that no DXSpider service already exists:

   sudo systemctl status dxspider.service

If a service exists, do not overwrite it: review its configuration and
operation. If none exists, create the file as a sudo-enabled user:

   sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/dxspider.service

Suggested contents for a new installation:

   [Unit]
   Description=DXSpider DX Cluster (mojo)
   Wants=network-online.target
   After=network-online.target

   [Service]
   Type=simple
   User=sysop
   Group=spider
   WorkingDirectory=/spider
   ExecStart=/usr/bin/perl -w /spider/perl/cluster.pl
   Restart=on-failure
   RestartSec=10
   StandardOutput=journal
   StandardError=journal

   [Install]
   WantedBy=multi-user.target

Before creating the service, check the group used by
the reference installer and the user group membership:

   getent group spider
   id -nG sysop


If the spider group does not exist, create it using sudo:

   sudo groupadd spider

If sysop is not a member, add it:

   sudo usermod -aG spider sysop

Do not run the service until both conditions have been
checked. Also verify that /usr/bin/perl is the correct path:

   command -v perl

If the path differs, adjust ExecStart to the
verified path. Check that no interactive cluster.pl remains running.

   sudo systemctl daemon-reload
   sudo systemctl enable --now dxspider.service
   sudo systemctl --no-pager --full status dxspider.service
   sudo journalctl -u dxspider.service -n 80 --no-pager

The service must not run as root.



