11. NEW COMMANDS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This section describes the implementation; it does not imply live testing.
Enter the following commands in the DXSpider console, NOT a Linux
terminal. The privileges shown are the minimum required by the code.

11.1 DXREG REGISTRATION (requires reg_enable and DXReg to be ready)

 request/register <CALL> <email> <EN|ES> [ssid-list]
     SYSOP (priv >= 9): requests registration for the specified CALL.
     ssid-list accepts 1, 1,2,5, 1-5 or 1,3-5,10.
     Example: request/register user@example.org ES 1,2-4
  show/register [CALL|ID]
     SYSOP (priv >= 9): no argument lists pending requests;
     CALL shows history; ID shows the request details.
 accept/register <ID|CALL> [note]
     SYSOP (priv >= 9): accepts a pending request and displays
     the resulting password, which must be kept confidential.
 reject/register <ID|CALL> [note]
     SYSOP (priv >= 9): rejects a pending request.
 remove/register <CALL> [note]
     SYSOP (priv >= 9): revokes the base callsign registration and
     existing SSIDs 1..99; preserves DXUser records, removes passwords,
     sets registered=0 and disconnects affected sessions.

 Administrative DXReg operations cannot be run remotely
 or from scripts. See README_DXReg for details.

11.2 BLOCKLISTS

  load/badnode
  load/badspotter
     Priv >= 9, not remote. These commands read the corresponding native
     DXHash list files, validate their entries and replace the in-memory state
     after a successful read. On failure, they preserve the previous
     in-memory state. Do not assume these commands write to SQLite.

 testbadip [suffix] <IP> [IP ...]
     Priv >= 6, not remote; requires active DXCIDR. Checks for
     a match in BadIP; returns (1) if matched or (0) otherwise.
     The optional suffix defaults to local. Example: testbadip local 192.0.2.1

 unset/badip [suffix] <IP-or-range> [others ...]
     Priv >= 6, not remote; requires active DXCIDR.
     Removes entries from the badip.<suffix> set (default: local)
     and reloads DXCIDR. Example: unset/badip
     local 192.0.2.0/24. Check the specifications accepted by
     DXCIDR::valid_spec.

11.3 ACCESS CONTROL

 set/nextlogin <seconds> <CALL> [CALL ...]
     SYSOP (priv >= 9), neither remote nor script. Sets the
     next permitted login time for existing DXUser records. A base
     CALL also affects existing SSIDs 1..99; CALL-SSID affects only
     that record. A value of 0 removes the
     restriction. Example: set/nextlogin 3600 EA3CV-2.
     Do not confuse this with immediately disconnecting active sessions.

11.4 MONITORING

 show/health [subcommand]
     SYSOP (priv >= 9). With no argument, equivalent to
     status. Options: status, connections, traffic, filtering, diagnostics,
     origins,
     bursts, pc92, peers [CALL], queues, web, rbn, topology,
     self_health and all.
     Examples:
        show/health
        show/health diagnostics
        show/health peers EA3CV-2
        show/health all
     Queries DXSupervisor/DXHealth telemetry. Availability and metric
     details depend on the active instrumentation.

